MiniMax-H3 video engine: Splash's own, and what it still copies from h3

Copied from h3 commit e6deb5e (branch main), as of its keyframe conditioning
(FL2VA first-frame and first-and-last-frame), without its history.

Splash's own: runtime/video. It began as h3's host code (its engine, model,
ops and media), which now runs on Splash's base layer and is laid out as
Splash and SGLang Diffusion lay out theirs:

  runtime/video/VideoRuntime.*   the engine process of a video model and the
                                 VideoRuntime, a pipeline of stages run in
                                 order on a request's batch (SGLang's
                                 ComposedPipeline)
  runtime/video/pipeline/        the stage interface and the batch
  runtime/video/stages/          TextEncodingStage, PrepStage,
                                 DenoisingStage (h3's scheduler and denoising
                                 loop), PreviewStage, DecodingStage,
                                 AudioDecodingStage, MuxStage (h3's mp4 writer)
  runtime/video/model/           the weights' readers, request preparation,
                                 the DiT's activation plan and weight stream,
                                 the text encoder
  runtime/video/ops/             the operators, which take their weights as
                                 buffers, and the shapes they take
  runtime/video/metal/           the dispatch recorder and the M5 Pro tuning

h3's C ABI is gone: the runtime calls the stages, and they the model and the
operators, directly; failures are exceptions, as Splash fails. On Splash's
base layer:

  h3 metal/MetalBackend: device, queue, metallib, pipeline cache, buffers and
    command buffers -> metal/MetalBackend, one per engine process.
    video/metal/Encoder.hpp records a command's dispatches as the operators
    bind them; a kernel that runs an exact grid takes
    ComputeDispatch::gridThreads. One command runs a DiT block, a VAE clip or
    a preview chunk, the next encoded while it runs.
  h3 model/Safetensors, model/Json and its pread loops -> model/
    SafetensorsCheckpoint (a converted directory read as one index),
    model/WeightSource and parallelFor (model/WeightImages.hpp); the
    decoders' manifests through NSJSONSerialization
    (video/model/WeightFiles.hpp).
  h3 engine/Status, Logger -> exceptions, and logLine on stderr.
  std::chrono::steady_clock -> AwakeClock.
  metal/Residency of Splash is a Metal residency set: it cannot express the
    DiT's SSD read-ahead ring, so video/model/WeightStream keeps its own.

Its data are Splash's too: the weight stream reads the int8 matrices from the
DiT's block files, which h3 copies into a stream image (stream.bin, 22 GB per
variant), and request preparation gathers the adaLN tables of the variant's
schedule from dit-<variant>/adaln.safetensors, which `splash video-adaln`
computes once at conversion with h3's own arithmetic, where h3 computes them
from FL2VA's transformer shards (26 GB read) at every request. The int8
weights' format is QUANT_VIDEO_W8 of runtime/metal/abi/QuantFormat.h, which
the readers check the manifests against; the images are served from a
repository of the engine's format (install/video.py), which
dev/tools/publish_video_repo.py makes with the converters.

Its development tools are under dev/: the synthetic DiT weights and their
fill kernel (dev/tests/engine/VideoSynthetic.*, video_synthetic.metal), and
the golden-clip tests' engine (dev/tools/video_golden.mm), which reads and
writes .npz files (dev/tools/Numpy.*); the engine process takes none of
their options.

Still copied from h3, with include paths rewritten for their places
("metal/abi/" -> "metal/abi/video/", "metal/kernels/common/" ->
"metal/kernels/video/common/"):

  h3 runtime/metal/kernels/GROUP/        -> runtime/metal/kernels/video/GROUP/
     (but shared/synthetic.metal; common/quant.h, whose format constants are
     metal/abi/QuantFormat.h's, is Splash's)
  h3 runtime/metal/abi/                  -> runtime/metal/abi/video/
     (but SyntheticABI.h; TextEncoderABI.h there is Splash's)
  h3 tools/convert_*.py, h3/{__init__,common,config,safetensors}.py
                                         -> dev/tools/video/

Their arithmetic is h3's: the golden clip of make test-video-real is byte for
byte h3's. To bring an upstream change, dev/tools/vendor_video.sh H3_CHECKOUT
copies these again and prints the upstream changes to h3's host code and
common/quant.h since the commit above, which are ported into runtime/video and
quant.h by hand; then record the
new commit here, rebuild, and run make test-engine-metal and make
test-video-real.

Beside them, Splash's kernels/video/text_encoder.metal (the prompt's text
encoder) and this note.

Build (Makefile): the kernels compile into splash.metallib as its video
group with Splash's production Metal flags, the prep kernels also without
fast math, as h3 builds them, since they reproduce MLX's arithmetic bit for
bit. The host code compiles with Splash's flags plus -ffp-contract=off, as h3
builds it, into build/engine/libsplash-video.a, which links before the
engine's library; the mp4 writer links AVFoundation, CoreMedia and CoreVideo.

